+**The voice from the body (2026-11-11 (120); `rules/voice-body.ts`, `rules/data/info-rate.ts`, `body-sheet.ts` bodyBlock, `rules-tools.ts` BodySize, `index.ts` typeUnit and showPlate).** A character's voice is computed from causes kept with the person, not stored as a sound: the player's picks (2026-11-11) are the full chain, bits per syllable from the 17 measured languages, and each person's own difference as one draw from a measured spread. The forward map is a function; its reverse is not (many tract shapes give one formant pattern: Atal et al. 1978), so the causes are kept.
+
+| Term | Definition |
+|---|---|
+| tract length | height × voiceTractPerHeight: men 0.1004, women 0.0965 (fitted: the tract length the formant spacing of Hillenbrand et al. 1995's 45 men and 48 women gives, c ÷ 2ΔF (Fitch 1997), over US adults' mean height, body-size.ts; Peterson & Barney 1952 give 0.1077 and 0.0998) |
+| formants | of the neutral vowel: F_n = (2n − 1) c ÷ 4L, c = 331.3 √(1 + 37 ÷ 273.15) = 353.0 m/s (a 17.5 cm tract: 504, 1513, 2522 Hz) |
+| fold length | membranous, mm: 0.18 h − 15.8 for men, 0.24 h − 28.1 for women (cadaver larynges, 22 men and 17 women) |
+| pitch | f0 = v ÷ 2L, the folds as a string: v, the transverse wave speed, fitted per sex to the measured mean speakers (men 4.225 m/s, women 4.938 m/s: 131.2 and 220.4 Hz); fold length alone gives 70 % of the sexes' log-pitch difference |
+| syllables a second | 39.15 bits/s ÷ the language's bits per syllable (Coupé et al. 2019; the 17 languages' mean for another), × 1.027 for a man or 0.973 for a woman, × (1 − 0.000794 × (age − 30.6)), × (1 + 0.0882 × rateZ), × 4.12 ÷ 5.08 for talk against reading (Kuhlmann & Iwarsson 2024); the sex, age and spread from the same data's 170 speakers |
+| rateZ | drawn once when BodySize places a person, from a random stream keyed to the chat and the name, so no other draw moves |
+
+| IF | THEN |
+|---|---|
+| a person has a height and a sex and is 18 or over | the tract, formants, folds and pitch are computed |
+| else | the rate only |
+| the box shows said words of a tracked person | they appear at that person's syllables a second (box.ts revealTimes, as a share of voiceSyllablesPerS) |
+| the narrator's [BODY] lists a placed person | a line "<name>'s voice: tract …, formants … Hz, pitch about … Hz, … syllables/s in talk" follows |
+| the plate names a tracked person | its hover shows the same line |
+
+Measured against: the rate so predicted lies within 16 % of each of the 17 languages' measured rates, 8.2 % root mean square (English 5.52 against 6.38 syllables/s read aloud; Thai 5.45 against 4.70). Within one sex the chain makes height decide the tract and the pitch wholly; measured voices show weaker links (across 39 samples tract-length estimates explain up to 10 % of the variance in height, pitch under 2 %: Pisanski et al. 2014; Künzel 1989, 105 men and 78 women, no significant correlation).
+
+Check: `tests/voice-body.test.mjs` V1 (the tube), V2 (the measured speakers' mean body), V3 (the rate), V4 (adults only; the narrator's notes), V5 (the wiring); negative controls VB1–VB10; Chromium (`st-chat.mjs … voice 0 light none on` with `RULES_PEOPLE`): Tom at rateZ −1 (4.18 syllables/s) said "Sit. Will has been counting…" in 2894 ms against 2871 computed (2400 at the unmarked 5), his voice on the plate's hover.
